<script>
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// QUESTION BANK  (20 questions, ~13 written / 7 MC)
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
const BANK = [

// ─── CHANNEL: ports ───────────────────────────────────────────
{id:'p1',ch:'ports',type:'w',
 q:'What are the four main instance attributes stored on a ConnectionPort? For each, give its Python type and what it means.',
 c:[{l:'port_id (str)',t:['port_id','str']},
    {l:'label (str)',t:['label']},
    {l:'parent_block (BaseBlock)',t:['parent_block','baseblock']},
    {l:'connected_to (Optional[ConnectionPort])',t:['connected_to','none','optional']}],
 m:`port_id (str) — unique name within the block, e.g. "CH1_IN"; used as the key in port_map during save/load.
label (str) — the short string painted near the circle on the canvas.
parent_block (BaseBlock) — the block this port belongs to; used in connect_ports() to reject self-loops.
connected_to (Optional[ConnectionPort]) — None when free; holds a reference to the peer port when connected. connect_to() sets both sides simultaneously.`
},

{id:'p2',ch:'ports',type:'mc',
 q:'add_port("VCC","VCC",0.5,0.0) is called on a block with BLOCK_WIDTH=280 and BLOCK_HEIGHT=240. Where does the port appear in local block coordinates?',
 snip:`<span class="cm"># base_block.py:189</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">add_port</span>(self, port_id, label, x_frac, y_frac):
    port.<span class="fn">setPos</span>(self.BLOCK_WIDTH * x_frac,
                self.BLOCK_HEIGHT * y_frac)`,
 opts:[
  {t:'x=140, y=0 — horizontal midpoint, top edge',c:true, w:'Correct. 280×0.5=140, 240×0.0=0. Top-centre is the conventional power-pin position.'},
  {t:'x=0, y=120 — left edge, vertical midpoint',c:false,w:'x_frac=0.5 maps to 140, not 0. x=0 would require x_frac=0.0.'},
  {t:'x=140, y=240 — horizontal midpoint, bottom edge',c:false,w:'y_frac=0.0 maps to 0 (top), not 240 (bottom).'},
  {t:'x=280, y=120 — right edge, vertical midpoint',c:false,w:'x_frac=0.5 → 140, not 280. 280 would require x_frac=1.0.'},
 ]
},

{id:'p3',ch:'ports',type:'w',
 q:'Why does ConnectionPort.__init__ call super().__init__(-r, -r, 2*r, 2*r, parent) with negative x and y? What does that mean visually?',
 c:[{l:'centre the circle on setPos point',t:['center','centre','centred','centered','origin','middle']},
    {l:'-r,-r shifts bounding rect so pos is centre',t:['-r','-6','negative','shift','offset']},
    {l:'QGraphicsEllipseItem rect is relative to local origin',t:['local','relative','bounding']}],
 m:`QGraphicsEllipseItem takes a bounding rect in the item's own local coordinates, with (0,0) at the top-left. By using (-r, -r, 2r, 2r) the circle's bounding rect is centred on the item's local origin. When setPos(x, y) is called, that origin lands exactly at (x,y) in the parent's coordinate system. Without the offset, the top-left corner of the circle (not its centre) would sit at the port position — the dot would appear shifted half a radius down and right of the block edge.`
},

{id:'p4',ch:'ports',type:'w',
 q:'The Oscilloscope block has BLOCK_WIDTH=300 and BLOCK_HEIGHT=220. It adds CH1_IN at x_frac=0.0, y_frac=0.15. What pixel position is that, and which edge of the block is it on?',
 snip:`<span class="cm"># oscilloscope_block.py:41</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> range(<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">5</span>):
    self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(f<span class="str">"CH{i}_IN"</span>, f<span class="str">"CH{i}"</span>,
                  <span class="num">0.0</span>, <span class="num">0.15</span> + <span class="num">0.2</span> * (i - <span class="num">1</span>))`,
 c:[{l:'x=0 (left edge)',t:['x=0','left','0.0 ×','0 px']},
    {l:'y=33 (0.15 × 220)',t:['y=33','33','0.15 × 220','0.15×220']},
    {l:'left edge of block',t:['left edge','left side']}],
 m:`x = 300 × 0.0 = 0 px → the left edge of the block.
y = 220 × 0.15 = 33 px → 33 pixels from the top.
So CH1_IN sits at the left edge, about 15% down from the top. CH2_IN would be at y = 220 × 0.35 = 77 px, and so on (each 0.2 × 220 = 44 px lower).`
},

// ─── CHANNEL: wires ───────────────────────────────────────────
{id:'w1',ch:'wires',type:'mc',
 q:'Why does WireItem call self.setZValue(-1) in __init__?',
 snip:`<span class="cm"># canvas.py:57</span>
self.<span class="fn">setZValue</span>(-<span class="num">1</span>)   <span class="cm"># render BELOW blocks</span>`,
 opts:[
  {t:'So the wire renders behind all blocks (ZValue 0), keeping port circles visible on top.',c:true, w:'Correct. Lower ZValue → painted first → underneath. Blocks default to 0, ports inherit the block\'s layer. Wires at -1 are always below both.'},
  {t:'So mouse events on the wire are suppressed, preventing accidental wire selection.',c:false,w:'ZValue controls render order, not hit-testing. Mouse-event suppression is done via setAcceptedMouseButtons/setFlag instead.'},
  {t:'So the wire appears dimmer than blocks, giving a visual hierarchy hint to users.',c:false,w:'ZValue is a depth/order property; it has no effect on colour, opacity, or brightness.'},
  {t:'So the wire is skipped during scene.items() iteration in _port_at().',c:false,w:'_port_at() skips the temp wire by identity check (item is self._temp_wire), not by ZValue.'},
 ]
},

{id:'w2',ch:'wires',type:'w',
 q:'connect_ports(src, dst) has two guards before it creates a WireItem. What are they, and what happens when each fires?',
 snip:`<span class="cm"># canvas.py:185</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">connect_ports</span>(self, src, dst):
    <span class="kw">if</span> src.parent_block <span class="kw">is</span> dst.parent_block:
        <span class="kw">return None</span>
    <span class="kw">if</span> src.connected_to <span class="kw">or</span> dst.connected_to:
        <span class="kw">return None</span>`,
 c:[{l:'self-loop guard: same parent_block → None',t:['same block','self-loop','parent_block','same parent']},
    {l:'already-connected guard: either port busy → None',t:['already','connected_to','busy','occupied']},
    {l:'both return None silently',t:['return none','none','silent','no exception','no wire']}],
 m:`Guard 1 — self-loop: if src.parent_block is dst.parent_block, the user tried to wire a block to itself. Returns None immediately; no wire or signal is emitted.
Guard 2 — overwrite protection: if either port already has a connected_to peer, wiring it again would orphan the existing connection without removing the old wire. Returns None; the existing connection is preserved.
Both failures are silent (no exception), so the UI simply stays unchanged.`
},

{id:'w3',ch:'wires',type:'w',
 q:'Describe exactly what _remove_wire(wire) does, in order. What state does it clean up and why does the order matter?',
 snip:`<span class="cm"># canvas.py:203</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_remove_wire</span>(self, wire):
    wire.src_port.<span class="fn">disconnect</span>()
    wire.dst_port.<span class="fn">disconnect</span>()
    self._wires.<span class="fn">pop</span>(wire.wire_id, <span class="kw">None</span>)
    self.<span class="fn">removeItem</span>(wire)
    self.wire_removed.<span class="fn">emit</span>(wire.wire_id)`,
 c:[{l:'port.disconnect() nulls both sides connected_to',t:['disconnect','null','none','connected_to','both side']},
    {l:'_wires dict is popped',t:['_wires','pop','dict','registry']},
    {l:'removeItem removes from scene',t:['removeitem','scene','remove']},
    {l:'wire_removed signal emitted',t:['signal','emit','wire_removed']}],
 m:`1. src_port.disconnect() — sets src.connected_to = None AND clears the peer's connected_to. Doing this before removeItem means the ports update their colour (back to blue) while the scene is still valid.
2. dst_port.disconnect() — symmetric; clears the other side. (In practice src.disconnect() already nulls both, but calling both is defensive.)
3. _wires.pop(wire.wire_id, None) — removes the WireItem from the scene's bookkeeping dict so it doesn't appear in to_dict() or future lookups.
4. removeItem(wire) — deletes the C++ QGraphicsLineItem from the scene.
5. wire_removed.emit — notifies listeners (e.g. status bar) that a wire is gone.
Order matters: if removeItem ran first, the C++ object would be invalid and calling disconnect() or accessing port references through the wire would crash.`
},

{id:'w4',ch:'wires',type:'mc',
 q:'update_position() is called on every WireItem during mouseMoveEvent. What does it do and when is it needed?',
 snip:`<span class="cm"># canvas.py:60</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">update_position</span>(self) -> None:
    src_pos = self.src_port.<span class="fn">scenePos</span>()
    dst_pos = self.dst_port.<span class="fn">scenePos</span>()
    self.<span class="fn">setLine</span>(<span class="cls">QLineF</span>(src_pos, dst_pos))`,
 opts:[
  {t:'Redraws the wire by querying current world-space port positions, keeping it taut when a block is dragged.',c:true, w:'Correct. scenePos() returns the port\'s current world coordinates. When a block moves, its ports move with it; the wire must re-query those coords to stay anchored.'},
  {t:'Animates the wire colour from gold to green to confirm a successful connection.',c:false,w:'update_position() only calls setLine(); it doesn\'t touch the pen or colour at all.'},
  {t:'Recalculates the ZValue so the wire stays under any blocks that overlap it.',c:false,w:'ZValue is set once in __init__ and never changes. update_position() only updates the line endpoints.'},
  {t:'Checks whether the two connected ports are still on the same block and deletes the wire if so.',c:false,w:'There\'s no validation in update_position(); it only reads scenePos() and calls setLine().'},
 ]
},

{id:'w5',ch:'wires',type:'w',
 q:'If you delete a WireItem from the scene (removeItem) WITHOUT calling port.disconnect() first, what breaks? Be specific.',
 c:[{l:'connected_to still set — ghost state',t:['connected_to','ghost','still set','not null','not none']},
    {l:'ports still green / show connected',t:['green','colour','color','_update_color','visual']},
    {l:'connect_ports() will refuse future connections',t:['connect_ports','refuse','guard','already','overwrite']}],
 m:`The two ports (src and dst) still hold references to each other via connected_to. This "ghost" state has three consequences:
1. The port dots remain green (COLOR_CONNECTED) even though no wire is visible, confusing the user.
2. connect_ports() guard 2 ("if src.connected_to or dst.connected_to: return None") fires — both ports appear "busy" so any future connection attempt to either port is silently rejected.
3. to_dict() looks for wires via self._wires, so the wire won't be serialised — but the ports still think they're connected, creating an inconsistency between the layout file and runtime state.`
},

// ─── CHANNEL: serial ──────────────────────────────────────────
{id:'s1',ch:'serial',type:'w',
 q:'Walk through how from_dict() reconstructs wire connections. What is port_map, what is its key, and what happens if a block fails to load?',
 snip:`<span class="cm"># canvas.py:333</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">from_dict</span>(self, data):
    self.<span class="fn">clear</span>()
    port_map = {}
    <span class="kw">for</span> bdata <span class="kw">in</span> data[<span class="str">"blocks"</span>]:
        block = self.<span class="fn">add_block</span>(bdata[<span class="str">"type"</span>], ...)
        <span class="kw">if</span> block:
            <span class="kw">for</span> port <span class="kw">in</span> block.ports:
                port_map[(bdata[<span class="str">"id"</span>], port.port_id)] = port
    <span class="kw">for</span> wdata <span class="kw">in</span> data[<span class="str">"wires"</span>]:
        src = port_map.<span class="fn">get</span>((wdata[<span class="str">"src_block"</span>], wdata[<span class="str">"src_port"</span>]))
        dst = port_map.<span class="fn">get</span>((wdata[<span class="str">"dst_block"</span>], wdata[<span class="str">"dst_port"</span>]))
        <span class="kw">if</span> src <span class="kw">and</span> dst:
            self.<span class="fn">connect_ports</span>(src, dst)`,
 c:[{l:'port_map key is (block_id, port_id) tuple',t:['block_id','port_id','tuple','(','key']},
    {l:'port_map maps to ConnectionPort objects',t:['connectionport','port object','port ref']},
    {l:'failed block → ports absent from port_map → wire silently dropped',t:['absent','missing','silent','drop','skip']}],
 m:`port_map is a dict keyed by (block_id, port_id) tuples — e.g. ("abc12345", "CH1_IN") → the live ConnectionPort object.
It is built in the first loop: for every successfully loaded block, each of its ports is registered with the block's saved id as part of the key.
If a block fails to load (add_block returns None — e.g. because device_type is not in BLOCK_REGISTRY), its ports are never added to port_map. In the wire loop, port_map.get(...) returns None for those keys, the "if src and dst" guard fails, and the wire is silently dropped — no error, no partial connection.`
},

{id:'s2',ch:'serial',type:'mc',
 q:'to_dict() serialises wires as {"src_block": ..., "src_port": ..., "dst_block": ..., "dst_port": ...}. What value is stored in src_port?',
 opts:[
  {t:'The port_id string, e.g. "CH1_IN"',c:true, w:'Correct. wire.src_port.port_id is the string set in add_port(). This is the key used in port_map during from_dict().'},
  {t:'The ConnectionPort Python object reference',c:false,w:'Python objects cannot be JSON-serialised. to_dict() serialises only the port_id string, not the object itself.'},
  {t:'The integer index of the port in the block\'s _ports list',c:false,w:'Index order is fragile — it changes if _setup_ports() order changes. port_id is the stable identifier used instead.'},
  {t:'The label string shown on the canvas, e.g. "CH1"',c:false,w:'label is for display only. port_id is the canonical identifier stored in the serialised wire.'},
 ]
},

{id:'s3',ch:'serial',type:'w',
 q:'A colleague loads a .sil layout and discovers that all wires connecting to a particular block vanished. Name two root causes that could produce this and explain the mechanism behind each.',
 c:[{l:'device_type mismatch — block not in registry',t:['device_type','registry','mismatch','block_registry','function gen']},
    {l:'port_id changed — port absent from port_map',t:['port_id','port_map','absent','missing','renamed']},
    {l:'both cause port_map lookup to fail → wires silently dropped',t:['port_map','silent','drop','none','missing key']}],
 m:`Cause 1 — device_type/registry mismatch: the block's device_type string doesn't match any key in BLOCK_REGISTRY (e.g. "Function Gen" vs "Function Generator"). add_block() returns None, so the block isn't created, its ports never enter port_map, and every wire referencing them is dropped.
Cause 2 — port_id rename: a developer changed _setup_ports() so that a port's id string changed (e.g. "CH1_IN" → "CH1IN"). The saved layout still refers to the old id. port_map.get((block_id, "CH1_IN")) returns None; the wire is dropped silently.
Both mechanisms share the same failure point: port_map.get() returns None, the "if src and dst:" guard fails, and connect_ports() is never called — no error, no wire.`
},

